      "text": "You are writing a review of *People We Meet on Vacation* by Emily Henry. Here is the information gathered to address your points:\n\n### 1. Publisher, Release Date, and Standalone Status\n\n*   **Publisher:** Berkley\n*   **Release Date:** 2021\n*   **Standalone Status:** The novel is a standalone contemporary romance.\n\n### 2. Premise and Main Characters\n\n*   **Premise:** The novel follows Poppy and Alex, best friends who have taken an annual vacation together for a decade. The story is told in alternating timelines that trace how their close friendship suddenly collapsed and whether they can now try to rebuild it.\n*   **Main Characters:** Poppy and Alex.\n\n### 3. Chapter Alternation\n\nThe book is celebrated for its **dual-timeline structure**, which alternates between:\n*   **The Present:** The \"now\" timeline, focusing on the attempt to reconnect during a final, crucial trip.\n*   **The Past:** Flashbacks to their previous nine summer vacations, tracing the evolution of their relationship.\n\n### 4. Opening Chapter Tone (Poppy's Narration)\n\nThe opening excerpt, which is clearly from Poppy's first-person perspective, immediately establishes a reflective, intimate, and slightly nostalgic tone. Key elements of her voice include:\n*   **Intimacy and Focus on Detail:** She focuses on a highly specific, shared memory (discovering Alex's genuine laugh) that serves as a foundational moment in their relationship.\n*   **Strong Relational Focus:** The narration is driven entirely by her assessment of Alex and their dynamic (\"He was going home... I was going home...\").\n*   **Foreshadowing:** The text ends by directly referencing the breaking point (\"This year, for the first time, we were not going to...\"), setting up the central conflict through an emotional lens.\n\nHere is the start of the opening chapter for your verification:\n\n> \"y for an entire summer. He was going home to East Linfield, Ohio. I was going home to Palmetto, Florida. We pulled up a map and drew the straightest possible line between the two towns, and then we crossed it out and drew a much crookeder line, because the first one went nowhere interesting.\n>\n> Alex drove. Alex always drove. I did the playlists and the snacks and the terrible jokes. Somewhere in a gas-station parking lot in western North Carolina, at three in the morning, I discovered that he had been laughing politely at me for six hours and finally, finally, made him actually laugh. He has a specific laugh that he only lets out when he is genuinely amused, a short, quiet, surprised laugh that begins with a pulled-in breath. It is the single most rewarding sound in the world. I have been chasing it ever since.\n>\n> That was nine summers ago. This year, for the first time, we were not going to...\"",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
